[{"id":"VRCE-2-61-1","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":1,"english":"And when the night had departed and the sun had arisen, those officers of the royal house-hold belonging to the twice-born order, Märkandeya, Maudgalya, Vāmadeva, Kaśyapa, Kātyāyana, Gautama, and the highly famous Jābāli. assembled together along with the counsellors, spoke each on different topics.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061001","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3470447bdf82c6e21dca4771ca3265925d0d56b47321759f1cb854d163b0e50d","compact":"242ef694089d5a9b1cfdfbed560e2af399acb2a940fba754ed728842e531e3c2","nasal":"242ef694089d5a9b1cfdfbed560e2af399acb2a940fba754ed728842e531e3c2"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-2","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":2,"english":"And when the night had departed and the sun had arisen, those officers of the royal house-hold belonging to the twice-born order, Märkandeya, Maudgalya, Vāmadeva, Kaśyapa, Kātyāyana, Gautama, and the highly famous Jābāli. assembled together along with the counsellors, spoke each on different topics.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.754,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061002","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"259a8147a50fb881d1f731c74eed37982f9b139b82df8bb9b56542af32dde251","compact":"d821020ae30f07f2a9e59d7aadd16938cf3cfd105ca0f1acc3ed35f922827ff6","nasal":"c6111f5ba02560c80c9bab332fcc2091b351f64c05648edac842388ec712e397"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-3","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":3,"english":"And when the night had departed and the sun had arisen, those officers of the royal house-hold belonging to the twice-born order, Märkandeya, Maudgalya, Vāmadeva, Kaśyapa, Kātyāyana, Gautama, and the highly famous Jābāli. assembled together along with the counsellors, spoke each on different topics.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.879,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061003","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a98cf294ffa5ba7cffe5acdcdd5a65a0e4bd6fb640ed744cfd901550aa85137f","compact":"7db791dbb79bf5228243ca73cd7a00f2c3ee067f52f8c3eb0604c00951ab9027","nasal":"7db791dbb79bf5228243ca73cd7a00f2c3ee067f52f8c3eb0604c00951ab9027"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-4","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":4,"english":"Then facing the royal priest, the eminent Vasistha, they said, \"That night that had appeared like to an hundred years has at last been painfully passed.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.871,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061004","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ec0ae283b0f8e6122a20a553e03a60e36247cc00d8e4748c746a649720057fc8","compact":"d8b457e61862c5caf04fd5113b67e07ae6d004c4e72f588091a507ac9f77bb31","nasal":"fc2df6bb9e9ef15ee3eec54f7bccb673fc4273f272b2edfb44463a6cca7cf9da"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-5","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":5,"english":"The king racked by sorrow for his son having breathed his last, the mighty monarch has ascended heaven, Rāma has taken refuge in the woods, the energetic Lakşmaņa has gone with Rama, and does Bharata and Satrughna, repressors of foes, are staying in Kekaya in pleasant Rājagrha, the abode of their maternal grandfather.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.754,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061005","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"892454e4644f12d3c1901767eb8ee91bf9da68e848b0c780a50153687d3135f7","compact":"3478d31c66d0e4d585adb62d746b425085fe052e94d5b307f22793c93dea8c2c","nasal":"3478d31c66d0e4d585adb62d746b425085fe052e94d5b307f22793c93dea8c2c"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-6","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":6,"english":"The king racked by sorrow for his son having breathed his last, the mighty monarch has ascended heaven, Rāma has taken refuge in the woods, the energetic Lakşmaņa has gone with Rama, and does Bharata and Satrughna, repressors of foes, are staying in Kekaya in pleasant Rājagrha, the abode of their maternal grandfather.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.846,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061006","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e2f88828f4f99975c13d61e3f8c5dea485ca243adfd50210dccc9beb03a190c9","compact":"dff6f034c98b1f24f8bd5abeb21c6ae8d6f8b0bda21f45f35fd4f3f31888cb7c","nasal":"dff6f034c98b1f24f8bd5abeb21c6ae8d6f8b0bda21f45f35fd4f3f31888cb7c"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-7","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":7,"english":"Do you select some on this very day from the descendants of Ikşvāku, to be king here. Verily does a kingdom go to ruin, when without a king.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.794,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061007","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f3ac6621ae74834a36973fbb57798c57a7b4e9877fcbfa3fde908be2828048cc","compact":"9ca42cc3678470bbfa0f5a009a3625b9cbcc65fc2e6bebc4627524666b347237","nasal":"9ca42cc3678470bbfa0f5a009a3625b9cbcc65fc2e6bebc4627524666b347237"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-8","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":8,"english":"He that goes garlanded with lightning, and has a mighty voice, even the Cloud, does not with Skye shower drench the Earth in a kingdom without a king.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.898,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061008","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3de752525d9746091c31b154b648b45a62cc43296fd4c86e3be36b94763ad2c0","compact":"3b10e11a20ac349b444a50d76ea58fd7e71f7d049ba50acab441116888280d0c","nasal":"3b10e11a20ac349b444a50d76ea58fd7e71f7d049ba50acab441116888280d0c"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-9","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":9,"english":"In a kingless kingdom no one sows corn. In a kingless kingdom the son does not obey his sire, or the wife her husband.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.8,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061009","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"41279f494058dabfef927d09e077c7d224ec2c0652c9c6c20eefd63b10b2b3f8","compact":"fef63a99218b997aab6e01e5a9eab8a6d535052c1c5f312028415c244e46678f","nasal":"fef63a99218b997aab6e01e5a9eab8a6d535052c1c5f312028415c244e46678f"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-10","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":10,"english":"A kingless kingdom possesses no wealth, and wives are hard to keep in such a place. This great fear attends a kingless country. And where is other morality (besides that detailed above) to be found at such a place?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061010","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2ca158edf34321e11d1740fe537ce3af0b8070145e4da373f7d33cf685cab45d","compact":"b7f92ee08d5009fd83868e2cb65cf2322b4da635c70d868e9c9c550beeee393f","nasal":"b7f92ee08d5009fd83868e2cb65cf2322b4da635c70d868e9c9c550beeee393f"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-11","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":11,"english":"In a kingless country men do not form themselves into associations, nor do they, inspired with cheerfulness, make elegant gardens or sacred edifices.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061011","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"699f2c3cbb7ed9b8a0ae467a3094a5ada7faea12ef2f2eb71e8f1d16c68d69a6","compact":"a8f1ac790fb6c27ad028b9be333378424ab45a2b977f54484fb4e710d154fd80","nasal":"ba0b772c06fa7f9f603067a21a1584d0d70456877833babb32533dee620e7021"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-12","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":12,"english":"In a kingless country, the twice-born ones do not celebrate sacrifices. In a kingless country, in mighty sacrifices wealthy Brāhmaṇas do not confer (on the officiating priests) the Dakșiņās (which they receive according to the ordinance).","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061012","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"23fbc91c2238ddda4cbf6606729d30c87ab6f8de3329e5f1bd2c066984651b35","compact":"80a6c07d8f5d375485dddbe63a24bbf7e5ffcfe22dbe848158fc97429bc7f757","nasal":"655202b856f59b5bb7898efae674ecaca456f421452c9a8177b4035abe7b352a"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-13","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":13,"english":"In a kingless country, neither neither social gatherings, nor festivities characterised by the presence of merry theatrical managers and performers, increase.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.831,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061013","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"21be5ca9a6e4cce22d6c002bdb27dbba78b1b3ca6e4c2d8810c4c28ffafbd6e4","compact":"55f45c586bc31cb3db08292386de527c711e00047a2c32973f1649761dba7dc3","nasal":"876711e88ae5b0f0102bd5060e41746ca492ef9ba273e98c708497472e66adb6"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-14","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":14,"english":"In a kingless country disputants cannot decide their point; nor are persons given to hearing Puranic recitations pleased by those delighting in the practice.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.793,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061014","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"fa77f437bb4fcbbceb44a0af832270ff9364aa7b0daa4408b51cd9aa53a7026d","compact":"c0e31d292f76a1717bca2fbe1a7ac192e26536cef47a1b1bbbf1e04a96eb8a50","nasal":"cc60aed5fcb55b7047b7e18db76446728945e9809097b9afd06ed9cf14218e93"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-15","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":15,"english":"In a kingless country pleasure-seeking people do not in company with females go to the woods by means of swift vehicles.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.831,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061015","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0b0234f67437177ec8416762a088529bb3dcb390abc4bcfeb38f7bbb8542ba0f","compact":"9cef6f332f7be823f3962468a7902eebf513e88f8b69c94c7b4cf8772a3e3210","nasal":"aeca395d94271305738e222b1cd029cae087ff8c57b4d7ea85f01cbb3d658689"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-16","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":16,"english":"In a kingless country, the wealthy are not well protected; nor do shepherds and cultivators sleep with their doors open.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.891,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061016","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"74a5739d29d9759665a3d9528e11466853aa20ed1f3a28f894acfb4a5b4690a3","compact":"0e5be7a971785bb55085743654c2c2f665fdbce92472cddad69c019059a6ca9e","nasal":"8d15c4dc8079497b1aa7e42a115ca108940bfb0e0230ed92dd11d74711a14914"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-17","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":17,"english":"In a kingless country traders coming from distant lands, loaded with various kinds of merchandise, do not with safety go along the roads.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.855,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-18"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061017","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5846ea424d50c2243f394b29a8420952b8aa64848d112179a45496ddf4963875","compact":"a61f8d853afd1d716507ec20afa52b914bf33ec40a2c2103122c8a3eca9ad3d7","nasal":"300b1ae07a67b01dc7a59146347cf058411a8045f7bb0f6a9bbf8a629044c7ec"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-18","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":18,"english":"In a kingless country the ascetic with his subdued senses, himself his sole protector, who makes his quarters wherever evening overtakes him, cannot walk contemplating the Deity.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-19"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061018","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"aac8c5ac4a846e8777a766046d6d177d429a518e51c729a3d3f47d53f5a5f495","compact":"dc558711fe9a1933676119fc3c1428e014422163f3bc1953f57c82fa888a0531","nasal":"50d59a4145081ebeb11796db6f08c0d6b6d03336af3df73926e133931e216012"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-19","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":19,"english":"In a kingless country, one cannot protect what one has, or procure what one has not. In a kingless country, the forces cannot bear the onslaught of the foe.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.855,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061019","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5fd7fd292b6646049411e4dc426298be6b2324f5b623e5bd7625374b1f55a13e","compact":"283de46e63a9fc9a7007813052374173ed53dd2f0083e4d4245acc690a4d1f67","nasal":"283de46e63a9fc9a7007813052374173ed53dd2f0083e4d4245acc690a4d1f67"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-20","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":20,"english":"Even as a river without water, a wood without grass, a herd of kine without a keeper, is a kingdom without a king.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-24"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061020","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b1efdfbecf5c417e56f0e03ed5c4ca8419d3f7eb76672b9771c09fd9e5acfd0b","compact":"ae35a65a9bb9f22fe66c47d9ac31f8316e449110a7802d1ad533196ccc250e53","nasal":"ae35a65a9bb9f22fe66c47d9ac31f8316e449110a7802d1ad533196ccc250e53"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-21","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":21,"english":"In a kingless country a person hardly preserves his life; and like fishes people eat up one another.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.842,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-26"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061021","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0613e2b10a0889804381f3455fffce95fad1de1e1817a886546ecf515bf7c7ce","compact":"1da13b180442d02db5d7351ce803361f6fc16d98cd4f1196d7faef9b9f00f8ec","nasal":"5b030eb5f1aff301ebdc11f5c663c19cfe609dcf9516d5e70b92c4b8a2418822"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-22","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":22,"english":"Even those heretics who having disregarded the dignity of social morality had met with chastisement at the hands of the king, their fear removed, give themselves airs.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.908,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-27"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061022","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3c55e81087f3da622b0da2d19b93f648d18672857d1c1c61cb4d75ba1dd67bc6","compact":"c8c6200f4e905d7b30f8f141559761521ee3fdf69cda394c2ac42c7fd75c7031","nasal":"95d4c2c5c28a792b33634d8420327b5785a44082b4d91b1ecbcd6277efabc14a"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-23","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":23,"english":"If there were not a king in this world to adjudge fair and foul, darkness would overspread (the face of the earth) and people could not distinguish anything whatever. As the ocean keeps within its continent, we even while the monarch lived, did not disregard your words.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.656,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-31"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061023","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"54d8ef1f90f0490bfab9229b6b4e2b572990b2258e48a75f40f51d53a1b9076c","compact":"77e1da8538701cc9fbe448ceae83163d1cf120f8840f581a045e2c1ca05d830c","nasal":"c18c6cd35348d27c02c06cd0e308f380ebb092bb9d747329d034825c24abe58e"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-24","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":24,"english":"If there were not a king in this world to adjudge fair and foul, darkness would overspread (the face of the earth) and people could not distinguish anything whatever. As the ocean keeps within its continent, we even while the monarch lived, did not disregard your words.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.964,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-31"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061024","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c1acc07899e59989b7275bed163558366bc5117aba2923b76febb987f3044beb","compact":"8747216a5029a4128f958636e5359bc378260c6645e7de1f0e6ab59408f982a7","nasal":"8747216a5029a4128f958636e5359bc378260c6645e7de1f0e6ab59408f982a7"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-61-25","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":61,"verse":25,"english":"Do you, O best of Brāhmaṇas, beholding our acts rendered nugatory, and this empire became a wilderness for want of a king, install that descendant of Ikşvāku or any other as king of this realm.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.736,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-67-32"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2061025","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"59566835a73f3b6a08b0788a6694474b1604b8a56c0983005d50ec8b0362653d","compact":"ca860d71faf50b715e091e28d9bd1158f3fbc78365f383929bf0fb7aa89e1f27","nasal":"77d35218bae1e742b2a3c87998800785caee47e8d1202943703d1955de6cfa7e"}}]
